Copy this file to `dossier/case-brief.md` (repo root, private — case data never
lives under `omnigent/`) and replace every placeholder below with your own case's
real identity. The coordinator prompt reads `dossier/case-brief.md` at the start of
every run to learn what case it is working; without it, the coordinator has no case
to investigate.
-->

# Case Brief — <Case Name>

## Authorisation & scope

State who authorised this investigation, in what capacity (e.g. private
investigation client, investigative-journalism outlet), and any explicit
boundaries: in-scope jurisdictions, what is out of bounds, PII-handling rules, and
OPSEC constraints (e.g. passive collection only — never contact or tip off
subjects).

*Example: "Authorised private investigation on behalf of [client], investigative-
journalism scope. In-scope jurisdictions: GB, US, NL. Passive collection only —
never contact subjects. No publication of unredacted minors' data."*

## Subject

One or two sentences: what is this investigation about, and what conduct or
question is being investigated?

*Example: "Investigation into the supply chain and corporate structure behind a
consumer product marketed under the ExampleBrand name."*

## Target entities

The starting set of companies/individuals known at case open — the seed the first
runs' leads will expand from.

*Example:*

| Entity | Jurisdiction | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Example Trading Ltd | GB | Principal trading entity |
| Example Holdings B.V. | NL | European operating arm |
| Jane Example | GB | Director, both entities (alleged) |

## Known affiliates / brands

Consumer brands, trading names, or affiliated entities already known to be part of
the same network at case open.

*Example: "ExampleBrand — the consumer-facing trading name used across the GB and
NL entities."*

## Open questions at case open

The starting questions the first leads should chase.

*Example:*
- Beneficial ownership of Example Holdings B.V. beyond the disclosed director.
- Whether Example Trading Ltd and Example Holdings B.V. share common control.
